`std.conv.parse` now converts decimal strings to `float` and `double` with correct rounding

$(REF parse, std, conv) and $(REF to, std, conv) now return the `float` or
`double` nearest to a decimal input, with ties rounded to even, matching
`strtod` and the floating-point parsers of other languages. Previously the
result could be off by one or two units in the last place, on all platforms
and for about half of all inputs on platforms where `real` is 64 bits wide,
such as AArch64 with LDC. $(MREF std, json) parses numbers through
`std.conv` and is fixed too. `real` is parsed this way when it has the same
format as `double`.

-------
import std.conv : to;

assert(to!double("0.3") == 0.3);
assert(to!double("1.7976931348623157e308") == double.max);
-------

Decimal inputs too small to be represented now parse as zero on all
platforms, as hexadecimal inputs already did, instead of throwing a
$(REF ConvException, std, conv) where `real` is 64 bits wide.
